WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.705
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.705 --> 00:00:04.410
I help some leaping
lizards track down

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.540
why their bandwidth
was off in the weeds.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:08.010
Will we catch
what's bugging them?

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.220
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:15.450
I got a nice call from the Gecko
Protocol team, a B2B company

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:18.300
offering a custom, lightweight
networking protocol, built

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.634
for gaming and other
real-time graphics systems.

00:00:20.634 --> 00:00:22.800
Their team was seeing lower
than expected throughput

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.230
for their back-end
machines, which

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.340
are responsible for
transferring and transcoding

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.530
large video and graphics files.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.930
Their architecture worked a
little something like this.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:32.970
A client will push about
two terabytes of data

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:33.999
to cloud storage.

00:00:33.999 --> 00:00:35.790
At the same time, it
kicks off work request

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.090
to a front end, which leverages
a TCP load balancer to spin up

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:42.000
a bunch of compute and engine
instances in a single region

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.280
to feed on that data,
talk between each other,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.020
and get the work done.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.920
Most of this was
working as expected.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.530
However, the Gecko
Protocol group

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.930
was concerned about VM
to VM throughput, where

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.530
they were only seeing about
1.5 gigabit per second max

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:56.490
connections.

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:58.110
I duplicated their
setup on my side,

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.270
which involved a bunch
of one-CPU machines

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:02.800
in a single region
and ran iPerf on them.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.629
The result was
about 1.95 gigabits

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:06.420
per second, which was
much higher than what

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.860
Gecko Protocol was seeing.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.500
Now, I knew from experience
that for high bandwidth systems,

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.390
they were obviously
using too small a machine

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.460
for those efforts, since
networking throughput

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:15.930
is related to core count.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:19.410
Bumping it up to about 16 CPU
machines in the US central one

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.410
region, I was able to
get about 15.9 gigabits

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.370
per second--
obviously, much better.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.495
However, when the Gecko
Protocol team switched over

00:01:26.495 --> 00:01:29.430
to this configuration, they
didn't see the same results.

00:01:29.430 --> 00:01:32.520
They only ended up at about
1.6 gigabits per second.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.440
After about 20 minutes
of a video chat trying

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.180
to get our
configurations identical,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.174
the solution smacked
us right in the face.

00:01:38.174 --> 00:01:40.590
The problem was that their
team was testing the connection

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.630
to the external
IP of the server,

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.547
while I was testing
on the internal IP.

00:01:45.547 --> 00:01:47.130
See, if two machines
are communicating

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:49.110
using their internal
IPs, then they

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:51.300
can send traffic on
Google's private internet,

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:52.710
which is super fast.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.260
Using the public IP, however,
forces the data packet

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:56.880
to get pushed to
the public internet

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.160
before coming back to
the machine, a.k.a.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.212
not the fastest.

00:02:00.212 --> 00:02:01.920
When we switched over
their configuration

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:04.710
to use the internal IPs with
the larger machine setup,

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.300
the bandwidth went
through the roof--

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.550
15.9 gigabits per second.

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.680
For Gecko Protocol, this
small debugging session

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:12.630
resulted in their
video and graphics data

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:15.270
transfer rate
improving by about 14x.

